 ## Overview
-This method implements the vertex shader stage of a GPU-accelerated 3D Gaussian Splatting rasterizer, specifically designed to compute 2D screen-space projections and basis vectors for instanced quad rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently transforming 3D Gaussian parameters (mean, covariance, opacity) into screen-space ellipses while performing early culling to reduce geometry shader load. It differs from CUDA-based variants by executing these calculations in the vertex shader stage, relying on subsequent geometry or fragment shaders for final pixel accumulation [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+This method implements the vertex shader stage of a differentiable 3D Gaussian splatting rasterizer, designed for high-performance rendering on GPU architectures that support geometry shaders or instanced rendering. It transforms 3D Gaussian parameters (mean and covariance) into screen-space 2D ellipses, computes the necessary basis vectors for quad generation, and performs early culling to optimize downstream processing. It differs from CUDA-based variants by offloading the heavy linear algebra and culling logic to the vertex/geometry shader pipeline, relying on texture buffers for data storage rather than linear memory layouts.
 
